Make innsegl init work, and stop it asking the human to sign (#158) (#159)

* fix(cli): resolve -repo to an absolute path before deriving the gitsign path (#117)

* fix(cli): force-add the verification marker past the repository's own gitignore (#117)

* fix(cli): stop init from configuring a human to sign (#158)

commit.gpgsign=true demanded of the operator exactly the credential the
settled design never gives them: only the orchestrator signs, through
sign_commit, with an MCP-issued identity, and the human never signs as
themselves. init no longer writes commit.gpgsign, and apply() never
backs it up or restores it — any pre-existing opinion about it is left
untouched. gpg.format and gpg.x509.program are still recorded: both are
inert without commit.gpgsign or an explicit -S, and they document which
pinned, verified gitsign this deployment trusts. The apply/undo and
VERIFIED reports now say plainly that the deployment can sign, not that
this repository signs.